{
  "_readme": "Hole coordinates for the club's own course. Re-record any hole with tools/map-course.html if the layout changes, and paste the result over that hole.",
  "_source": "Hole centre-lines, greens and bunkers from OpenStreetMap, (c) OpenStreetMap contributors, under the Open Database Licence (ODbL) - https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright. Derivation: 'path' is the whole golf=hole way, tee first; 'tee' its first node; 'center' the centroid of the matching golf=green polygon; 'front' and 'back' that polygon's nearest and furthest vertices from the tee. Every hole matched its own green to within a metre, which is the check that the matching worked.",
  "_cardYards": "Measured tee to green centre in a straight line, so the nine total 2,634 against the 2,825 printed on the white card: the card measures from the back tee along the line of play. Club off the live 'Distance to green' numbers, which are measured from where you actually stand - not off this.",
  "_par": "Par 35, which is what the published card says. OpenStreetMap tags par on eight of the nine; the 7th is untagged there and is a par 5 on the card, agreeing with the stroke indexes, where 6 is the one missing.",
  "_note": "Each hole can carry a 'note': local knowledge no commercial app has for this course - where the trouble is, what the wind does, which side to miss on. Nobody has written these yet. Add them by hand, one or two sentences, from someone who plays it.",
  "_verify": "The geometry is survey data, not a club walk-round. Check a few holes against the card and pace one green: that is the difference between this being the course and it being close to the course. Until that is done 'verified' stays false and the Yardage tab carries a line saying so, which is the honest state to publish in.",
  "mapped": true,
  "_verified": "Set this true in the edit that records the walk-round, and only then: it removes the 'not checked on the ground yet' line from the Yardage tab. 'mapped' says there are real coordinates here; 'verified' says a person has stood on the course and agreed with them.",
